\description{
Assigns vaginal microbiome samples to community state types (CSTs) with
VALENCIA (France et al. 2020): each sample goes to the most similar of 13
reference centroids (sub-CSTs), by Yue and Clayton's (2005) similarity of
relative abundances.
}
\details{
For each sample, the relative abundances \eqn{p} are its counts divided
by its total. Its similarity to each centroid \eqn{q} is Yue and
Clayton's \eqn{\theta}, over the union of the sample's and the centroid's
taxa (a taxon missing from either is 0 there):
\deqn{\theta = \frac{\sum p q}{\sum (p - q)^2 + \sum p q}}
It is 1 when \eqn{p = q}, and 0 when they share no taxa. Taxa that the
centroids don't name still count, through \eqn{\sum p^2}, and lower
every similarity. The sample's sub-CST (\code{type}) is the centroid of
largest similarity (the first in the centroids' order, on ties), and its
\code{score} is that similarity. Sub-CSTs are then grouped into CSTs (\code{cst}):
I-A and I-B into I, III-A and III-B into III, and IV-C0 to IV-C4 into
IV-C.

\strong{The community state types} (France et al. 2020):
\itemize{
\item \strong{I} (I-A, I-B): dominated by \emph{Lactobacillus crispatus}; almost
entirely in I-A, less so in I-B.
\item \strong{II}: dominated by \emph{L. gasseri}.
\item \strong{III} (III-A, III-B): dominated by \emph{L. iners}.
\item \strong{IV-A}: high to moderate BVAB1 and \emph{Gardnerella vaginalis}.
\item \strong{IV-B}: high to moderate \emph{G. vaginalis} and \emph{Atopobium vaginae}.
\item \strong{IV-C}: few \emph{Lactobacillus}, \emph{G. vaginalis} or BVAB1: IV-C0, an even
community with \emph{Prevotella}; IV-C1 to IV-C4, dominated by
\emph{Streptococcus}, \emph{Enterococcus}, \emph{Bifidobacterium} or \emph{Staphylococcus}.
\item \strong{V}: dominated by \emph{L. jensenii}.
}

\strong{Scores.} VALENCIA's authors compare each sub-CST's scores with those of
their reference samples (the means and standard deviations in the
\code{types} attribute, and in \code{plot(x, "scores")}): a sample scoring far
below its sub-CST's reference fits it poorly.
}
\section{Taxon names}{

Features are matched to the centroids' taxa by their exact names, so
name them as VALENCIA does (see \code{colnames(valencia_centroids)}):
\itemize{
\item species of \emph{Lactobacillus}, \emph{Gardnerella}, \emph{Prevotella}, \emph{Atopobium} and
\emph{Sneathia} as \code{Genus_species}, such as \code{"Lactobacillus_crispatus"},
\code{"Gardnerella_vaginalis"};
\item other taxa summarized to genus, or a higher rank, with a prefix:
\code{"g_Streptococcus"}, \code{"f_Enterobacteriaceae"}, \code{"o_Micrococcales"};
\item a few names of their own: \code{"BVAB1"}, \code{"BVAB2/BVAB3"}, \code{"BVAB_TM7"}.
}

Sum features that share a name first (duplicated names are an error).
\code{valencia()} warns when \code{counts} has none of the taxa that dominate the
centroids (\emph{L. crispatus}, \emph{L. iners}, \emph{L. gasseri}, \emph{L. jensenii},
BVAB1, \emph{G. vaginalis}, and the genera \emph{Streptococcus}, \emph{Enterococcus},
\emph{Bifidobacterium} and \emph{Staphylococcus}), since a misnamed one leaves
samples in the wrong type; the \code{matched} column shows how much of each
sample was matched. \emph{Prevotella} species are often named differently:
VALENCIA's authors suggest merging them all into \code{"g_Prevotella"}, with
centroids merged the same way (\code{centroids}). VALENCIA's
\code{convert_qiime.py}, which renames QIIME 2 taxonomies, has no equivalent
here.
}

\section{Differences from Valencia.py}{

\itemize{
\item Valencia.py divides counts by a \code{read_count} column, which can exceed
the sum of the taxa; \code{valencia()} divides by each sample's sum unless
\code{totals} gives the read counts.
\item A sample of total 0 has \code{NaN} scores in both, and a type of \code{NA} here,
with a warning.
\item A sample that shares no taxa with any centroid scores 0 against all of
them. Valencia.py then assigns it to the first, I-A, with score 0;
\code{valencia()} does the same, with a warning.
\item The similarities are computed over each sample's non-zero taxa only,
as \eqn{\sum pq / (\sum p^2 + \sum q^2 - \sum pq)}, which equals
Valencia.py's form to rounding.
}
}

